'''
=== DISJOINT SET UNION (DSU / Union-Find) - Theory & Notes ===

What is DSU?
- A data structure that tracks elements split into non-overlapping (disjoint) groups/sets.
- Two main operations:
    1. Find(u)     → Who is the leader/root of u's group?
    2. Union(u, v) → Merge the group of u with the group of v.

---

Core Components:
    parent[] → Each node points to its parent. Root points to itself.
    rank[]   → Approximate height of the tree (used in Union by Rank).
    size[]   → Number of nodes in the group (used in Union by Size).

Initialization:
    parent[i] = i   → Every node is its own leader.
    rank[i]   = 1   → Every tree has height 1.
    size[i]   = 1   → Every group has 1 member.

---

Optimization 1: Path Compression (in findParent)
    - While finding the root, rewire every node on the path to point directly to the root.
    - Flattens the tree so future finds are nearly O(1).
    - Example:
        Before:  0 ← 1 ← 2 ← 3  (chain, O(n) to find root of 3)
        After:   0 ← 1, 0 ← 2, 0 ← 3  (flat, O(1) for all)

Optimization 2: Union by Rank
    - When merging two groups, attach the SHORTER tree under the TALLER tree.
    - Keeps the overall tree height small.
    - If both trees have equal rank, pick either as root and increment its rank by 1.

Optimization 3: Union by Size
    - When merging two groups, attach the SMALLER group under the LARGER group.
    - Update the size of the new root: size[root] += size[other].
    - Advantage over rank: you can always query the exact size of any group.

With both optimizations combined:
    Time per operation: O(α(n)) where α = inverse Ackermann function.
    α(n) ≤ 4 for any practical input size → effectively constant time.

---

Common DSU Problem Patterns:

    Pattern                    | Example Problems
    ---------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------
    Connected Components       | Number of connected components, Number of Islands
    Cycle Detection (Undirected)| If find(u) == find(v) BEFORE union(u,v) → cycle exists
    Kruskal's MST              | Sort edges by weight, union if not in same set
    Dynamic Connectivity       | "Are A and B connected after adding edge X?"
    Account Merge              | Group accounts by shared emails
    Redundant Connection       | Find the edge that creates a cycle
    Making Network Connected   | Minimum cables to connect all computers
    Number of Provinces        | Count distinct groups in a friend network

---

When to Use DSU vs BFS/DFS:

    Use DSU when...                                    | Use BFS/DFS when...
    ---------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------
    Edges arrive incrementally (online)                | You have the full graph upfront
    You need fast "are these connected?" queries       | You need to traverse/explore paths
    You're building a Minimum Spanning Tree (Kruskal's)| You need shortest path or ordering (topo sort)
    The problem is about merging groups                | The problem is about exploring neighbors

---

Cycle Detection with DSU (Undirected Graphs Only):
    - Before doing union(u, v), check if find(u) == find(v).
    - If YES → u and v are already in the same group → adding this edge creates a cycle.
    - If NO  → safe to union, no cycle from this edge.
    - NOTE: This does NOT work for directed graphs (use DFS with in_recursion for that).

---

Time & Space Complexity:
    Time:  O(α(n)) per find/union operation (nearly constant)
    Space: O(n) for parent, rank, and size arrays

Memorable Analogy:
    "Think of companies merging. Each company has a CEO (root).
     When two companies merge, the smaller CEO reports to the bigger CEO (union by size).
     Whenever any employee asks 'who is my CEO?', you update the org chart
     so they report DIRECTLY to the CEO (path compression) — no middle managers needed."
'''